; Duke Dungeon — the endless descent: a map drawn from a seed every time it is played.
;
; Nothing here is a floor. It is what the generator is asked for, so the same seed is
; the same floor on every machine, and a new seed is somewhere nobody has been. Killing
; the boss opens the way down; each depth is the same generator with a new seed and
; tougher inhabitants, and the hero keeps what he has earned, so the two curves have to
; be tuned against each other.
ProceduralMap
  Name = Endless

  ; How big a floor is and how many chambers it is cut into. What the ground
  ; between them is like — ragged or smooth, winding or straight, hills or level —
  ; is the Terrain of the theme the depth wears: see data/world/themes/.
  Generation = Layout
    ; Cells across and down. About four times the floor this used to be (50 by 36):
    ; a floor is now somewhere to go through rather than a room to clear, and the
    ; biomes coming next need space to be more than one of. Still no bigger than
    ; the chambers fill -- MaxRoomSpacing below keeps them together, and space
    ; nothing reaches just stays rock. Kept inside the 100 by 76 the 3D client
    ; carries today, which is deep.map's size, and deep.map stays the larger.
    MapWidth = 96
    MapHeight = 72

    ; MinRooms is what the generator aims for, and it can only be a number that
    ; actually fits: chambers are placed by trying and rejecting, and one that will
    ; not go in is one the floor does without. Sixteen is what always fits at these
    ; sizes -- the file must not claim a floor it cannot keep, and
    ; DungeonGeneratorTest holds it to its word. MaxRooms stays under deep.map's 28,
    ; the stage that is there to be bigger than any floor of the descent.
    MinRooms = 16
    MaxRooms = 26
    ; The footprint a chamber is grown in, in cells across. The chamber itself is
    ; rounder and rougher than its footprint, and never bigger.
    MinRoomSize = 7
    MaxRoomSize = 13

    ; Rock left between footprints, so two chambers grow apart rather than into one.
    RoomGap = 1
    ; How many times to try landing a chamber before settling for the ones that fit.
    ; Grown with the floor: the fuller it gets, the more throws the next one takes.
    PlacementAttempts = 8000

    ; How wide a tunnel is, in cells — and so the narrowest anything on the floor is
    ; allowed to be: a cell is 10 world units and the boss is 16 across, so one cell
    ; would wedge it. A mover with nowhere to swerve is how units used to end up
    ; inside walls, so this is a correctness setting rather than a matter of taste.
    CorridorWidth = 2

    ; How far a new chamber may be from the nearest one already placed, in cells.
    ; Keeps the floor compact: chambers scattered to opposite corners are joined by
    ; long empty tunnels, which are the part a player walks rather than plays.
    MaxRoomSpacing = 30

    ; Per chamber other than the hero's. Every occupied one gets at least the minimum.
    MinSkeletonsPerRoom = 2
    MaxSkeletonsPerRoom = 6
  End

  ; ---- which depth wears which ----
  ;
  ; Two floors out of doors, then two of stone, then round again. Out of doors
  ; first because the first floor is the one everybody sees and nobody chose to
  ; see -- the descent reads better as going in than as going further in.
  ;
  ; A theme is the ground as well as the look: the wood is open and rolling, the
  ; cellar close and cut. See the Terrain in each file under data/world/themes/.
  ;
  ; WhenExhausted is the answer to a real question in a game with no bottom:
  ; Repeat begins the list over, Last stays in the deepest look for ever.
  Themes = [Forest, Forest, Dungeon, Dungeon]
  WhenExhausted = Repeat

  ; ---- biomes: several places on one floor ----
  ;
  ; Listed, a floor stops wearing one theme whole and mixes these instead, the way
  ; a Minecraft world lays out its taigas and its deserts: two broad sweeps of
  ; weather cross the floor -- how wild, how alive -- and every cell grows the
  ; biome whose Climate (in its own file) is nearest the weather there. A chamber
  ; is one biome throughout, cut to that biome's Terrain; a tunnel between two is
  ; as like one as the other; and the hills rise as each region says without a
  ; cliff where two meet. Left out, the floor is the depth's theme above, exactly
  ; as it always was.
  ;
  ; Each cell is drawn in its own biome's kit (engine 0.7.0 asks the floor what
  ; every cell wears); what stands about and walks is dressed as the biome the
  ; heroes come in to. MapPicture washes each region in its biome's colour. Take
  ; the line out and every floor wears one theme whole again, by depth, exactly
  ; as before.
  Biomes = [Forest, Autumn, PineWood, DeadLand, Dungeon, Mine]
  ;
  ; About how many cells across one sweep of weather is -- so how big a region
  ; comes out. Forty gives three to five places on a floor of 96 by 72.
  BiomeSize = 40
  ; How far the whole floor's weather drifts per floor down, wild then alive: the
  ; woods thin and the worked, barren places -- mines, dead land -- gather deeper.
  ClimatePerDepth = [-6, -5]

  ; ---- what stands about in the rooms ----
  ;
  ; A room with nothing in it is a floor with a fight on it. These are ordinary
  ; templates (data/props/) with a shape and no body, which means the engine makes
  ; them solid — paths go round them, bodies stop at them — and neither brain will
  ; ever shoot at one, because both look for a candidate with a body. How each is
  ; drawn is the theme's business: a ThemeMonster in its Monsters re-skins any
  ; template, and a prop is a template. A theme that names no model for one gets
  ; the engine's plain shape, which for a cylinder is a pillar already.
  ;
  ; Fewest and most per room, including the one the hero starts in. Kept low:
  ; things to walk round are furniture, and a room full of furniture is a room
  ; you cannot fight in.
  PropsPerRoom = [0, 3]

  ; ---- the boss's keep ----
  ;
  ; Every floor of the descent ends in one: a square court a storey above the
  ; ground, walled round, its gate in the middle of the side a straight road and
  ; a stair come up to. Built in solid rock beside the deepest chamber with room
  ; for it, so it cuts nothing off -- see gen/Keep and
  ; docs/plan/2026-09-30-boss-keep.md. The boss waits in its middle and its
  ; guard round it. Stages are cut without one. Leave the block out and the boss
  ; waits in the furthest chamber, open, as it always did.
  Keep = Keep
    ; Across, walls included, largest first: the larger ones are tried only
    ; beside the deepest chambers, the last beside any. Odd, so the boss and the
    ; gate each have a middle cell.
    Sizes = [15, 13, 11, 9]
    ; What stands in its doorway until it is opened: data/props/gate.duke.
    Gate = Gate
    ; What it is drawn as, whatever biome it stands in: data/world/themes/keep.duke.
    Look = Keep
  End

  Descent = Descent
    ; ---- where it ends ----
    ;
    ; One boss per floor, in order, and this list is ALSO how many floors there
    ; are: kill the last of them and the run is won. There is no second number
    ; saying how deep the dungeon goes, because a second number is a number that
    ; can disagree with this one.
    ;
    ; Naming none puts the descent back the way it was: one boss called Boss, the
    ; same fight every floor, going down for ever. That is what this game did
    ; before it had an ending, and what a floor is worth depends entirely on the
    ; difference -- a descent with no bottom asks only how much further, and four
    ; named things to get past is a route you can finish.
    Bosses = [Warden, Reaper, Necromancer, Champion]

    ; ---- who stands with them ----
    ;
    ; A kind and how many of it. A kind not yet at its MinDepth stays away. The healer
    ; and the summoner are both at MinDepth 1, so no floor is without them: their guard
    ; stands with every boss, the first floor's included, and that is meant -- the green
    ; and the purple mages, two to mend the boss and two to fill the room while they do.
    ; They stand on a square ring BossGuardRing cells out from the boss, corners first,
    ; and further out if the room has no floor there. MaxPerRoom is for rooms the draw
    ; fills and does not apply here. Leave BossGuards out and every boss waits alone.
    BossGuards = [SkeletonHealer = 2, SkeletonSummoner = 2]
    BossGuardRing = 2

    ; Percentages are of the creature's base value, added per depth beyond the
    ; first: at depth 3 with 25%, a monster has 1 + 2 x 0.25 = 1.5x health.
    MonsterHealthPercentPerDepth = 25
    MonsterDamagePercentPerDepth = 15
    MonsterCountPercentPerDepth = 20

    ; The boss climbs faster than its underlings — it is meant to be the wall.
    BossHealthPercentPerDepth = 40
    BossDamagePercentPerDepth = 25

    ; Experience is worth more the deeper it was earned, so levelling roughly keeps
    ; pace with what it is levelling against.
    ExperiencePercentPerDepth = 30
  End
End
